| SET-UP CONTEXT | PREVENTIVE (SCHEDULED) MAINTENANCE | CORRECTIVE MAINTENANCE |
|----------------|-------------------------------------|------------------------|
| Job request | Scheduled maintenance task from the maintenance calendar or asset register. Specifies systems due for routine cleaning, inspection, update, and backup verification. Frequency may be monthly, quarterly, or as per organisational policy. | Unscheduled task triggered by a user fault report, event log alert, or failed hardware. The job order describes the fault symptoms, affected system, and priority. Corrective work begins after preventive checks or when a new fault is reported. |
| Tools and parts | Compressed air, soft brush, thermal paste, isopropyl alcohol, lint-free cloth, screwdriver set, anti-static strap, and temperature/diagnostics software. No replacement parts expected unless inspection reveals a fault. | Same cleaning tools plus spare parts (fan, RAM, disk, PSU), USB bootable media, and diagnostic software. Parts are drawn from inventory based on the diagnosed fault. |
| Report | Maintenance report listing date, asset ID, tasks performed (cleaning, updates, backup check), findings, and sign-off. No parts used unless a fault was found during inspection. | Maintenance report listing date, asset ID, fault description, corrective actions taken, parts replaced (with serial numbers), test result, and sign-off. |


| Type of Tools | Description |
|---------------|-------------|
| Compressed air / blower | Used to remove dust from vents, heatsinks, fans, and inside the chassis without touching components. Hold fans still while blowing to avoid overspinning and damaging bearings. Use short bursts and do not use near eyes or mouth. In dusty environments, schedule cleaning more often. |
| Soft brush (anti-static) | Used with compressed air to loosen dust from heatsinks and fan blades. A small brush helps reach tight spaces. Anti-static brushes reduce risk of ESD when working near boards. Never use a vacuum directly on the motherboard as suction can generate static and damage components. |
| Thermal paste / compound | Applied between the CPU and heatsink to improve heat transfer. Degrades over time; reapply when reseating the heatsink or after several years. Use a small pea-sized amount in the centre; spread evenly or let pressure distribute. Excess paste can cause mess and poor contact; clean old paste with isopropyl alcohol before reapplying. |
| Isopropyl alcohol (IPA) and lint-free cloth | Used to clean old thermal paste from the CPU and heatsink before applying new paste. High concentration (90%+) evaporates quickly and leaves no residue. Wipe gently; allow to dry before applying new paste and reseating the heatsink. |
| Screwdriver set and anti-static strap | Same as in set-up: open chassis, remove heatsink, and secure components. Anti-static strap is worn and connected to a grounded point when handling RAM, boards, or CPU to avoid ESD damage during corrective maintenance. |
| Temperature and diagnostics software | Used to check CPU, GPU, and disk temperatures and to view SMART data and event logs. Tools such as HWiNFO, SpeedFan, and Windows Event Viewer help identify overheating or failing hardware before failure and support evidence for maintenance reports. |


