| Hardware Component | Description |
|--------------------|-------------|
| Dust and airflow management | Dust blocks vents and heatsinks and causes overheating. The system should be powered off and unplugged before cleaning. Use compressed air and a soft brush; avoid vacuum directly on boards to reduce static risk. Clean fans, vents, and heatsinks according to the maintenance schedule (monthly in dusty environments, quarterly otherwise). Proper airflow depends on unobstructed vents and correctly routed cables inside the chassis. |
| Thermal paste and heatsink | The compound between the CPU and heatsink that improves heat transfer. It degrades over time and should be reapplied when reseating the heatsink or after several years of use. To replace: power off and unplug, disconnect CPU fan cable, remove heatsink, wipe old paste from CPU and heatsink with isopropyl alcohol and lint-free cloth, apply a small pea-sized amount of new paste to the centre of the CPU, reseat heatsink firmly and evenly, reconnect fan cable, and verify temperatures after power on. |
| Fans and cooling | CPU fan, case fans, and GPU fan keep components within operating temperature. Monthly checks ensure fans spin freely and are not noisy. Worn bearings cause noise and reduced airflow; replace the fan if noise persists after cleaning. Use BIOS/UEFI or temperature software to verify temperatures are within specification. |
| BIOS / UEFI firmware | Firmware that controls boot and hardware settings. Updates can fix compatibility and security issues; follow the manufacturer procedure and back up current settings before updating. A failed update can make the system unbootable. Check for updates during scheduled maintenance if the manufacturer has released a security or stability fix. |


| Software Component | Description |
|---------------------|-------------|
| Operating system updates | Security and cumulative updates applied as per organisational policy (e.g. weekly or monthly). Schedule outside peak hours to minimise disruption. Verify that updates install correctly and that the system boots normally after reboot. Roll back updates if they cause instability. |
| Antivirus definitions | Virus definition files must be kept current. Verify definitions are up to date during each scheduled maintenance. Scan results and quarantine actions are recorded in the maintenance report. Outdated definitions leave the system vulnerable to new threats. |
| Event log and diagnostics | The OS and hardware keep logs (e.g. Windows Event Viewer, SMART for disks) that record errors and warnings. Review these during scheduled maintenance to identify recurring faults, plan preventive actions, and document corrective maintenance. Monthly review catches failing components before complete failure. |
| Backup and restore tools | Software for creating and verifying backups of user data and system state before corrective maintenance. Windows Backup, macOS Time Machine, or third-party tools create restore points and full backups. After corrective maintenance that involves OS reinstallation or disk replacement, backups are used to restore user files and settings. Verify backup integrity before relying on it for recovery. |

![Annual Preventive Maintenance Schedule](images/maintenance-schedule.png)

| Maintenance Report | Description |
|--------------------|-------------|
| Date and asset | Date of maintenance; asset ID or serial number; location. Recording the date and asset identifier enables tracking of maintenance history per asset over time. This information also supports warranty claims by providing evidence of when and where maintenance was performed. |
| Type of maintenance | Preventive (scheduled) or corrective; reference to schedule or job order if applicable. Distinguishing preventive from corrective maintenance helps management assess the effectiveness of the maintenance schedule. Over time, a high ratio of corrective to preventive work may indicate that the schedule needs to be revised or that equipment is nearing end of life. |
| Tasks performed | List of tasks (cleaning, parts replaced, updates applied, tests run). A detailed task listing enables trend analysis across multiple maintenance cycles and supports planning of future preventive schedules. Consistent documentation also ensures that another technician can review what was done and continue follow-up work if needed. |
| Parts and materials | Part numbers and quantities used; none if preventive only. Recording parts supports inventory management by tracking stock consumption and triggering reorder when levels are low. This data also assists with warranty claims and enables cost tracking per asset for budgeting purposes. |
| Sign-off | Technician name and date; user or supervisor acceptance if required. Sign-off provides accountability by confirming who performed the work and when it was completed. It also confirms that the system was tested and returned to the user in working condition. |


| Common Fault | Cause | Action |
|--------------|-------|--------|
| Overheating / thermal shutdown | Dust block, failed fan, dried thermal paste | Clean vents and heatsink; replace fan; reapply thermal paste |
| No POST / no display | Loose RAM, faulty RAM, loose GPU | Reseat RAM and GPU; test with one stick; replace if faulty |
| Random crash / BSOD | Faulty RAM, driver issue, disk error | Run memory test; update or roll back driver; check disk SMART and event log |
| No boot / corrupted OS | Failed disk, bad update, malware | Repair or reinstall OS; replace disk if SMART failed; restore from backup if available |
| Noisy fan | Worn bearing, dust accumulation | Clean fan and surrounding area; replace fan if noise persists |
| USB / device not working | Loose cable, missing driver, port fault | Reseat cable; reinstall driver; try another port |


